CHOWN(1)                       P32W Unix                        CHOWN(1)

NAME
       chown - change the owner and group of files

SYNOPSIS
       chown owner[:group] file ...
       chown :group file ...
       chown owner: file ...

DESCRIPTION
       chown makes owner the owner of each file. owner is a login
       name from /etc/passwd or a numeric user id. If :group is
       given the group is changed too, to a group name from
       /etc/group or a numeric group id; otherwise each file keeps
       its group. With :group alone only the group is changed; with
       owner: (nothing after the colon) the group becomes the
       owner's login group from /etc/passwd.

       A name is looked up first; a string of digits that is not a
       name is taken as a number. An unknown name is an error and
       no file is changed.

       Only root may change owners. A file that already has the
       owner and group asked for is left alone, without error, so
       anyone may do that.

EXAMPLES
       chown ken notes.txt           give a file to ken
       chown guest:staff /usr/guest  owner and group together
       chown :staff report           the group only
       chown 100 a.out               by number

FILES
       /etc/passwd    login names and user ids
       /etc/group     group names and ids

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file was changed; 1 if owner or group is unknown,
       or if any file could not be changed (chown says why and goes
       on); 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       chgrp(1), chmod(1), ls(1), id(1), chown(2), passwd(5),
       group(5)

BUGS
       There is no -R (chgrp(1) has one, for the group).
